    "verdict": "no_meaningful_diff",
    "hypothesis": "Ruled out systematic differences across: math reasoning, riddles/trick questions, instruction-following and constrained formats (JSON, \"exactly N items\", \"no extra text\"), wordplay translation, logical paradoxes, roleplay, empathetic support, refusals/safety, factual recall, and length control. Both self-identify identically, follow constraints equally well, and produce the same class of occasional factual hallucinations (each erred on different questions — e.g., A mislabeled Dred Scott as 1848 while B garbled its 1920s Hitler entry; both made distinct errors on a 1920s-events prompt), which is consistent with sampling noise from the same underlying model rather than a systematic capability or stylistic gap. Surface variation (word choice, formatting, minor accuracy lapses) reproduced in both directions and did not track either model consistently.",
    "confidence": 85,
    "key_evidence": [
      "Deliberately contrasted accuracy probes failed to isolate one model: on 'List 5 historical events in the 1920s', model_A claimed '1924: The Chinese Exclusion Act is repealed' (wrong — that's the Immigration Act; repeal was 1943) while model_B claimed '1923: Adolf Hitler is appointed head of the Bavarian government' (wrong — Beer Hall Putsch) and '1926: The Ford Model T is discontinued' (1927). Errors appeared in both models on different facts.",
      "Earlier hint of an A-accuracy gap did not reproduce: A's 1848 answer contained errors (Dred Scott, Indian Mutiny) where B was better, but on later fact-dense prompts (moons of Mars, One Hundred Years of Solitude, circulatory system, 1920s) both performed comparably and both erred.",
